# Pocketa Clean Architecture Guide

## Project Structure

Pocketa follows Clean Architecture with clear separation of concerns:

```
lib/
├── core/                    # Core functionality
│   ├── constants/          # App constants
│   ├── errors/             # Error handling
│   ├── forms/              # Form management
│   ├── providers/          # Base Riverpod providers
│   ├── responsive/         # Responsive utilities
│   ├── theme/              # App theming
│   └── utils/              # Core utilities
├── features/               # Feature modules
│   ├── onboarding/         # Onboarding feature
│   ├── dashboard/          # Dashboard feature
│   ├── transaction/        # Transaction feature
│   ├── categories/         # Categories feature
│   └── wallets/            # Wallet feature
├── shared/                 # Shared functionality
│   ├── services/           # Business services
│   ├── ui_components/      # Reusable UI components
│   └── widgets.dart        # Widget exports
└── main.dart              # App entry point
```

## Feature Module Structure

Each feature follows the same Clean Architecture structure:

```
features/[feature_name]/
├── data/                   # Data layer
│   ├── models/             # Data models (Hive + JSON)
│   └── repositories/       # Repository implementations
├── domain/                 # Domain layer
│   ├── entities/           # Business entities
│   ├── repositories/       # Repository interfaces
│   └── usecases/           # Business use cases
└── presentation/           # Presentation layer
    ├── pages/              # Screen pages
    ├── widgets/            # Feature widgets
    └── viewmodels/         # State management (Riverpod)
```

## Key Principles

### 1. Dependency Inversion
- High-level modules don't depend on low-level modules
- Both depend on abstractions (interfaces)
- Use dependency injection with Riverpod providers

### 2. Single Responsibility
- Each class has one reason to change
- Clear, focused responsibilities
- Easy to test and maintain

### 3. Separation of Concerns
- **Data Layer**: Handles data sources and repositories
- **Domain Layer**: Contains business logic and entities
- **Presentation Layer**: Manages UI and user interactions

## File Naming Conventions

- **Entities**: `[name]_entity.dart` (e.g., `transaction_entity.dart`)
- **Models**: `[name]_model.dart` (e.g., `transaction_model.dart`)
- **Repositories**: `[name]_repository.dart` (interface), `[name]_repo_impl.dart` (implementation)
- **Use Cases**: `[action]_[entity].dart` (e.g., `get_transactions.dart`)
- **Providers**: `[feature]_providers.dart` (e.g., `transaction_providers.dart`)
- **Pages**: `[name]_screen.dart` (e.g., `dashboard_screen.dart`)
- **Widgets**: `[name]_widget.dart` (e.g., `transaction_card.dart`)

## Import Organization

```dart
// 1. Dart/Flutter imports
import 'dart:async';
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

// 2. Third-party packages
import 'package:flutter_riverpod/flutter_riverpod.dart';
import 'package:hive/hive.dart';

// 3. Core imports
import 'package:pocketa/core/core.dart';

// 4. Feature imports
import 'package:pocketa/features/transaction/domain/entities/transaction_entity.dart';

// 5. Shared imports
import 'package:pocketa/shared/ui_components/ui_components.dart';
```

## Base Classes and Patterns

### BaseEntity
All entities extend [BaseEntity](mdc:lib/core/data/base_entity.dart):
```dart
abstract class BaseEntity {
  String get id;
  DateTime get createdAt;
  DateTime get updatedAt;
  bool get isDeleted;
}
```

### BaseRepository
All repositories implement [BaseRepository](mdc:lib/core/data/base_repository.dart):
```dart
abstract class BaseRepository<T extends BaseEntity> {
  List<T> all({bool includeDeleted = false});
  T? getById(String id);
  Stream<List<T>> watchAll();
  // ... other methods
}
```

### BaseProviders
Use [BaseProviders](mdc:lib/core/providers/base_providers.dart) for common provider patterns:
```dart
final repositoryProvider = BaseProviders.repositoryProvider<Repo, Entity>(
  (ref) => RepoImpl(ref.watch(boxProvider)),
);
```

## State Management with Riverpod

- Use `StateNotifierProvider` for complex state
- Use `Provider` for simple values
- Use `StreamProvider` for reactive data
- Use `Provider.family` for parameterized providers
- Always extend `BaseStateNotifier` for state notifiers

## Error Handling

- Use [Result<T>](mdc:lib/core/result/result.dart) pattern for operations that can fail
- Handle errors with [ErrorHandler](mdc:lib/core/error_handling/error_handler.dart)
- Use specific failure types from [core/errors/](mdc:lib/core/errors/)

## Testing Structure

```
test/
├── core/                   # Core functionality tests
├── features/               # Feature tests
├── shared/                 # Shared functionality tests
└── widget_test.dart       # Widget tests
```

## Performance Guidelines

- Use `const` constructors wherever possible
- Implement `OptimizedWidget` for expensive widgets
- Use `MemoizedWidget` for expensive computations
- Follow responsive design patterns from [core/responsive/](mdc:lib/core/responsive/)